#{[17592186534036 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534036 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534036 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534036 #edb/ref 1071 "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation ${x}^{2}-1=k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534036 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98eaa55b-cc71-5317-b6aa-57c2d90c8064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534036 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =k \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k+1} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{k+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+1 & =0 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-1$ will give only one solution to the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534038 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534038 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111798" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534038 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534038 #edb/ref 1071 "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation  ${x}^{2}+8=k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534038 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9dc4d198-f79f-5ff0-bdf7-8f507199b081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534038 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8 & =k \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k-8 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k-8} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{k-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-8 & =0 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=8$ will give only one solution to the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-222450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534042 #edb/ref 1071 "If $4{y}^{2}+12y+9=0,$ then $y=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2d7658e-2fb7-5107-b38d-4768d5865883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534042 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}+12y+9 & =0 \\\\ (2y)^{2}+2\\cdot 2y\\cdot 3+{3}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (2y+3)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(2y+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |2y+3| & =0 \\\\ 2y+3 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534052 #edb/ref 1069 "q-23862" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534052 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534052 #edb/ref 1071 "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation $(1+3x)^{2}-5=k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534052 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b60332b2-78d9-552d-bf76-3110fd3f37e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534052 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+3x)^{2}-5 & =k \\\\ (1+3x)^{2} & =k+5 \\\\ \\sqrt{(1+3x)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k+5} \\\\ |1+3x| & =\\sqrt{k+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{k+5} & =0 \\\\ k+5 & =0 \\\\ k & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-5$ will give only one solution to the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534060 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534060 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534060 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534060 #edb/ref 1071 "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation ${t}^{2}+k-8=-2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534060 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1d7b383-1862-5cf5-aff4-6a1ed42adc9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534060 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we solve for $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+k-8 & =-2 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =-2-k+8 \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{6-k} \\\\ |t| & =\\sqrt{6-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-k & =0 \\\\ k & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=6$ will give only one solution to the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534081 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534081 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534081 #edb/ref 1069 "q-222457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534081 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534081 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation ${v}^{2}-4a=16.$ For which values of $a$ does this equation have no real solutions?\n\n$a$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534081 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df298759-b302-529c-87f4-be55ef966373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534081 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2}-4a & =16 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16+4a \\\\ \\sqrt{{v}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16+4a} \\\\ |v| & =\\sqrt{16+4a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n\n$$\n16+4a<0\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+4a & <0 \\\\ 4a & <-16 \\\\ a & <-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $a$ that is less than $-4$ will give an equation with no real solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534085 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534085 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534085 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534085 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $9{t}^{2}+30t+25=0,$ what are the values of $t?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534085 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eec9dcf0-bc54-564e-8800-178ded8aabf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534085 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the left-hand side is a perfect square. It just needs to be factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{t}^{2}+30t+25 & =0 \\\\ (3t)^{2}+2\\cdot 3t\\cdot 5+{5}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3t+5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(3t+5)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3t+5| & =0 \\\\ 3t+5 & =0 \\\\ 3t & =-5 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534086 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534086 #edb/ref 1069 "q-222406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534086 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534086 #edb/ref 1071 "The solution to $-6(3u-8)^{2}=0$ is $u=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534086 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eed297ec-4147-52da-9849-c80c4b4539e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534086 #edb/ref 1074 "We can solve this equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(3u-8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (3u-8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{(3u-8)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\ |3u-8| & =0 \\\\ 3u-8 & =0 \\\\ 3u & =8 \\\\ u & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534097 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534097 #edb/ref 1069 "q-23911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534097 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534097 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $(2x+2)^{2}-1=k.$ For which values of $k$ does this equation have no real solutions?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534097 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f96546d8-5860-5de5-ad05-79159b9add34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534097 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+2)^{2}-1 & =k \\\\ (2x+2)^{2} & =k+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(2x+2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k+1} \\\\ |2x+2| & =\\sqrt{k+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n\n$$\nk+1<0\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+1 & <0 \\\\ k & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $a$ that is less than $-1$ will give an equation with no real solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
